### Describe the problem that you experienced

It seems that the usage of signals outside of a form to drive form rules is supported, but I want to be sure that is intentionally the case outside of one part of the API.

The interop doc mentions the following pattern with [`SignalFormControl`](https://angular.dev/guide/forms/signals/migration#disabling-enabling-control):

```ts
export class UserProfile {
  readonly isRequired = signal(false);

  readonly emailControl = new SignalFormControl('', (p) => {
    // The control becomes required whenever isRequired is true
    applyWhen(
      p,
      () => this.isRequired(),
      (p) => {
        required(p);
      },
    );
  });
}
```

Similarly, I have personally noticed that following works for a regular signal `form()`, though I have not seen this mentioned explicitly in the docs or by others.

```ts
  loading = this.myResource.isLoading();

  readonly emailForm = form(this.model, (path) => {
    applyWhen(
      path,
      () => this.loading(),
      (p) => {
        disabled(p.email);
      },
    );
  });
```

You can go as far as make schema functions that take signals

```ts
  emailSchemaWithLoading(loading: () => boolean) {
	  return schema<Email>((path) => {
	      applyWhen(
		      path,
		      () => loading(),
		      (p) => {
		        disabled(p.email);
		      },
	    );
  }

  loading = this.myResource.isLoading();

  const signalForm = form(formModel, (schemaPath) => {
    apply(
      schemaPath.profile,
      emailSchemaWithLoading(() => this.loading()),
    );
  });
```



