https://angular.dev/guide/forms

### Describe what you were looking for in the documentation

If this behavior is intentional for a regular `form` itself.

### Describe the actions that led you to experience the problem

Having various use cases for having some state external to the form drive form state such as `disabled` (like page loading) or `required` (some state which is not in a form but otherwise drives if a field is needed, etc).

### Describe what you want to experience that would fix the problem

 I imagine the breakdown of what constitutes form state vs external state is an important consideration, but I think that a mention of this behavior and its supposed intentionality would be good to have noted somewhere. Besides just one example in the interop page.

### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

compiler-cli, compiler

### Is this a regression?

No

### Description

NG8011 (`controlFlowPreventingContentProjection`) reports a node as not being projected into its slot when there's an `@let` declaration next to it at the root of a control flow block, even though the node does end up in the right slot.

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'form-field',
  template: `
    <div class="control"><ng-content /></div>
    <div class="error"><ng-content select="[error]" /></div>
  `,
})
export class FormField {}
```

```html
<form-field>
  <input />
  @if (hasError()) {
    @let message = 'Email is required';
    <span error>{{ message }}</span>
  }
</form-field>
```

The compiler warns that `<span error>` won't be projected into the `[error]` slot because the `@if` has more than one node at its root, but at runtime the span is rendered inside `.error`. Removing the `@let` (and inlining the value) makes the warning go away without changing the output.

The compiler skips `@let` declarations when it looks for the single root node of a control flow block (since #58607), so the projection works. The diagnostic does its own count in `TcbControlFlowContentProjectionOp.shouldCheck` (`packages/compiler/src/typecheck/ops/content_projection.ts`), which only skips whitespace-only text, so it counts the `@let` as a second root node.

I'll send a PR with a fix and a test.

### Please provide a link to a minimal reproduction of the bug

No hosted reproduction, but this is self-contained (compiled with `ngc` and `strictTemplates: true`):

```ts
import {Component, signal} from '@angular/core';
import {bootstrapApplication} from '@angular/platform-browser';

@Component({
  selector: 'form-field',
  template: `control: [<ng-content />] error: [<ng-content select="[error]" />]`,
})
export class FormField {}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  imports: [FormField],
  template: `
    <form-field>
      <input />
      @if (hasError()) {
        @let message = 'Email is required';
        <span error>{{ message }}</span>
      }
    </form-field>
  `,
})
export class App {
  hasError = signal(true);
}

bootstrapApplication(App);
```

The build prints NG8011 for `<span error>`, and the page shows the message inside `error: [...]`.

